---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Link from '../../components/Link.astro';
import { routes } from '../../lib/links';
import { EDITORIAL_TEAM, type BreadcrumbItem } from '../../lib/schema';

const breadcrumbs: BreadcrumbItem[] = [
  { label: 'Home', route: routes.home() },
  { label: 'Editorial policy', route: routes.editorialPolicy() },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Editorial Policy | How Statohub guides and calculators are checked"
  description="How Statohub guides are researched, written and reviewed, which references the maths is checked against, how the calculators are tested, and how we use AI."
  canonicalPath={routes.editorialPolicy()}
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
>
  <article class="max-w-3xl">
    <p class="page-kicker">Editorial policy</p>
    <h1 class="page-title">How we decide what is true enough to publish.</h1>
    <p class="page-lede">
      This page is the standard every guide and calculator on Statohub is held
      to: where the maths is checked, what a review has to catch, how errors are
      corrected, and where AI tools are and are not used.
    </p>

    <div class="article-prose">
      <p><strong>Last updated:</strong> September 20, 2026</p>

      <h2>Who is accountable</h2>
      <p>
        Everything on Statohub is published by the
        <strong>{EDITORIAL_TEAM.name}</strong>, the group that commissions,
        writes, reviews and maintains the site. We publish under a team byline
        rather than individual names because every page passes through more than
        one pair of hands before it goes live, and the team as a whole answers
        for it. Who we are and why the site exists is on the
        <Link to={routes.about()}>About page</Link>. If something on a page is
        wrong, the address to write to is on the
        <Link to={routes.contact()}>contact page</Link>.
      </p>

      <h2>What we publish, and for whom</h2>
      <p>
        Statohub explains statistical methods and gives you a calculator to run
        them. It is written for students, self-learners and working analysts who
        need to understand a method well enough to use it and to know when it
        will mislead them. Every guide is expected to cover three things: what
        the method is, one example worked all the way through in real units, and
        the situations in which the method breaks down.
      </p>
      <p>
        We do not publish gambling or betting-odds tools, invented evidence, or
        anything that stands in for medical, legal or financial advice. The
        <Link to={routes.about()}>About page</Link> explains each of those rules.
      </p>

      <h2>Where the maths is checked</h2>
      <p>
        Formulas, definitions and worked examples are checked against primary
        references rather than against other websites. The references we return
        to most often, and cite from the guides themselves, are:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          the <a href="https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/">NIST/SEMATECH
          e-Handbook of Statistical Methods</a> for definitions, formulas and
          reference values;
        </li>
        <li>
          Penn State's online course notes, in particular
          <a href="https://online.stat.psu.edu/stat501/">STAT 501 (Regression
          Methods)</a>, for regression and correlation;
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics/pages/1-introduction">OpenStax
          Introductory Statistics</a> for the introductory material.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        When a guide gives a number, that number is computed, not quoted. Worked
        examples are recalculated by hand and, wherever the page has a
        calculator, cross-checked against the calculator's own engine so that the
        prose and the tool cannot disagree. Where a guide uses data to
        demonstrate a method, it is presented as a worked scenario and never
        dressed up as a real-world finding.
      </p>

      <h2>How the calculators are verified</h2>
      <p>
        Each calculator is a small, self-contained function maintained in this
        site's own codebase, not a third-party widget. Every function is covered
        by an automated test suite that has to pass before the site can be
        deployed. The tests compare results with values worked out
        independently and deliberately include the awkward inputs that produce
        quietly wrong answers elsewhere: empty lists, a single value, ties,
        negative numbers, and the sample-versus-population distinction.
      </p>
      <p>
        The calculators run entirely in your browser and nothing you type is
        sent to us. Results are shown with the intermediate steps so you can
        follow the arithmetic rather than take the final number on trust. A
        calculator is still a calculator: check that the method fits your data
        before you rely on its output.
      </p>

      <h2>What a review has to catch</h2>
      <p>
        Every draft is reviewed against a fixed written checklist before it is
        published. A draft that fails comes back with a specific list of fixes,
        not a general note. The checklist covers, among other things:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>the arithmetic in every worked example, recomputed;</li>
        <li>whether the page answers its own question and stays on one topic, so it does not duplicate another page on the site;</li>
        <li>whether the failure modes and edge cases a reader will actually hit are covered, not just the happy path;</li>
        <li>that no study is cited that has not been read, and no statistic is quoted that cannot be sourced;</li>
        <li>that every internal link resolves to a live page (the site will not build otherwise);</li>
        <li>that the frequently-asked-questions block has one entry per genuinely distinct question, with answers that add something rather than restate it.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How we use AI</h2>
      <p>
        We use AI tools for research and for proofreading only: to find and
        summarise source material, and to catch typos, unclear sentences and
        inconsistencies in a finished draft. The guides are written by the
        editorial team, every worked example is recomputed by the team, and
        nothing is published without a team review. AI output is never used as
        a source of facts or figures on its own.
      </p>

      <h2>Commissioned guides</h2>
      <p>
        Some guides in the <Link to={routes.appliedLanding()}>Applied</Link>
        section are commissioned from outside subject-matter writers. They are
        not published as they arrive: each one is edited for house style,
        checked for accuracy and for overlap with existing coverage, and only
        then published under the same byline and to the same standard as the
        guides written in-house.
      </p>

      <h2>Advertising</h2>
      <p>
        Statohub is funded by advertising served by Google AdSense, which is
        what keeps every guide and calculator free. Ads are selected by Google,
        not by us, and advertisers do not see, commission or influence any
        editorial content. How advertising cookies work here is set out in the
        <Link to={routes.privacyCookiePolicy()}>privacy and cookie policy</Link>.
      </p>

      <h2>Dates, updates and corrections</h2>
      <p>
        Every guide and calculator page shows two dates: when it was first
        published, and when it was last reviewed. The review date moves when the
        page is edited or when it has been checked again without needing
        changes. A page is revisited when we find an error, when an explanation
        is not landing, or when a better example replaces a generic one.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you find a mistake, tell us. Corrections to the maths are treated as
        urgent. When something substantive is corrected, the page is fixed, its
        review date changes, and we would rather hear about it from you than
        leave it up. The <Link to={routes.contact()}>contact page</Link> says
        what to include so we can find and fix the problem quickly.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
